/-
  OIBridge/SubstratumSource.lean — the substratum-source audit, first target: the three
  primitive-source principles collapsed onto one substratum object.

  ROUND SIXTY-ONE. The primitive-source audit (rounds 56-59) settled which structure selects
  quantum mechanics one level below the five operational conditions: reversible implementation
  locality, elementary transition richness, and embedded observation. The next question is
  whether that structure follows from the concrete physical realization of observation
  incompleteness, rather than from bare observation incompleteness as an abstract principle.
  This file takes the first step: it shows the three primitive-source principles are jointly
  supplied by ONE substratum object — an implementation architecture with four stability
  properties and one drivability property — and it isolates the single property that carries
  the physical content.

  THE OBJECT. An IMPLEMENTATION ARCHITECTURE (`Architecture`, round 59) is a class of
  admissible implementation operators closed under the operations every finite operational
  theory performs. A QUANTUM ARCHITECTURE (`QuantumArchitecture`) is one that is, in addition,

      · CONTEXT-STABLE — `𝓘 S K → 𝓘 (R × S) (1_R ⊗ K)` (round 57);
      · LABEL-INVARIANT — admissibility transported along carrier bijections (round 57);
      · DAGGER-STABLE — `𝓘 S K → 𝓘 S Kᴴ` (round 58);
      · ELEMENTARY-DRIVING (`DrivesElementary`) — at every carrier the transition flows, the
        state exchanges, and the quarter phases are admissible.

  THE COLLAPSE. A theory generated by a quantum architecture carries all three primitive-source
  principles at once: reversible implementation locality from the four stabilities
  (`genTheory_reversibleImplementationLocality`), elementary transition richness from
  drivability (`genTheory_elementary`), and embedded observation from label invariance
  (`genTheory_embeddedObservation`). Hence, on every nonempty finite carrier, a theory
  generated by a quantum architecture is exactly finite endomorphic operational quantum
  mechanics (`genTheory_qm_of_quantumArchitecture`), and finite operational QM is generated by
  one — the full class (`fullClass_quantumArchitecture`,
  `qm_generated_by_quantumArchitecture`). The physical question "which conditions select QM"
  becomes the single question "does the substratum supply one implementation architecture that
  is context-, label-, and dagger-stable and drives the elementary transitions".

  THE DECISIVE PROPERTY. Elementary drivability carries the content that the three stabilities
  do not. The DIAGONAL class is an architecture that is context-, label-, and dagger-stable
  (round 59) but NOT elementary-driving: the exchange of two distinguishable states is an
  off-diagonal operator, not admissible (`diagClass_not_drivesElementary`). Its generated
  theory is not quantum mechanics — it has no composite unitary control
  (`diagGen_not_quantumArchitectureGenerated`). So a substratum could supply a fully stable
  implementation architecture and still fail to be quantum; what it must additionally provide
  is a continuously drivable off-diagonal reversible transition between distinguishable states,
  the state exchanges, and a phase — the concrete controllability the abstract stabilities do
  not entail.

  WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. This does not derive a quantum architecture from A1-A6 or from the
  read-write substratum dynamics; it reduces the three primitive-source principles to one
  object and names its decisive property, so that the remaining audit is whether the concrete
  OI physics supplies that object. The minimal drivability repertoire is that of round 59 and
  is not reduced further here. Nothing here bears on the open redundancy of the inverse clause.
-/

import OIBridge.LieRankSource

